[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.520]   This is a Kell blog post from October 3rd, 2006 titled "10 Geek Business Myths."
[00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.840]   I found this excellent post, entitled "Top 10 Geek Business Myths" on Ron Garrett
[00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.920]   formerly Aaron Gatt's blog today. Garrett worked early on at Google,
[00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.320]   wrote for Exoglars, which appears defunct, and has become a venture capitalist. Here are his
[00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:23.840]   10 myths. Next, a brilliant idea will make you rich. Next, if you build it they will come next,
[00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.520]   someone will steal your idea if you don't protect it next, what you think matters next,
[00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.200]   financial models are bogus next, what you know matters more than who you know next,
[00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:36.240]   a PhD means something. Next, I need five of them to start my business. Next, the idea is the most
[00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.640]   important part of my business plan. Next, having no competition is a good thing. Next,
[00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.720]   fans bonus myth, after the IPO you'll be happy. Here are some of my thoughts on his myths,
[00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:50.160]   organized by number. One, it's rarely just the idea, it's the execution, and don't forget the
[00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.120]   timing. You need all three for big success. For example, business objects had a good product,
[00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:00.080]   with one awesome idea, the semantic layer, but we had great execution and near perfect timing.
