[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200]   This is a Kell blog post from October 3rd, 2006 titled "Deconstructing Databases."
[00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.760]   Here's a post where O'Reilly's Dale Daugherty writes about a talk at EuroOS Con by Greg Stein
[00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.960]   of Google Code, where Stein talks about building a bug tracking system.
[00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.120]   Traditional applications have been built on a traditional database view.
[00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.560]   That view requires that everything be decomposed into square tables.
[00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.560]   When you do this, you invariably end up making lots of fine-grained fields into which information
[00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:30.080]   should be placed -- e.g., bug number, short description, full description, assigned to, fixed in,
[00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:35.680]   related to, error number, severity, impact, etc. As it turns out, MarkLogic built its own bug
[00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:40.400]   tracking system, BugTrack, based on a special-purpose DBMS platform that has rich full-text
[00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.200]   searching capabilities, i.e., MarkLogic Server. And, given that assumption for the underlying
[00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:50.080]   platform, we did something similar to Google. Per E&Small, our VP of products, it's interesting
[00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:54.480]   to see how application development itself changes when you change the underlying platform assumption.
[00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.200]   In my mind, you end up with more powerful, more flexible applications in so doing. See the
[00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.360]   comments on Dale's post for more flexible applications in so doing. This audio version
[00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.560]   was created by Grant Duncan and AI, with permission from Dave.
