[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600]   This is a Kellblad post from October 4, 2006 titled "Fingerprinting Content."
[00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.480]   I recently ran across a cool sister Sequoia-backed company called Port Authority Technologies that
[00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.560]   sells information leak protection solutions. They sell an appliance that sits next to your
[00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:19.600]   firewall and looks, at a network level, for content that's getting sent outside that shouldn't be.
[00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.880]   What kind of content might that be? Contracts, marketing plans,
[00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:27.040]   draft financial press releases, source code, customer databases, and such.
[00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.720]   On discovering them, I first wondered, where were they when HP needed them?
[00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.560]   If you have not yet heard, it seems that Patricia Dunn and three other people involved in the HP
[00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.720]   affair are going to be indicted. It seems clear that monitoring outbound network traffic is a
[00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.960]   much better way of protecting information than calling phone companies under false pretexts.
[00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.560]   Then, always in touch with my inner marketer, I dreamed of the PR they could be generating
[00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.600]   on the back of the HP scandal. Then, I connected the dots on a series of companies using what I
[00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.640]   call "content fingerprinting technology" for interesting applications.
[00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.000]   Content fingerprinting is about scanning content, recognizing and remembering at a deep level,
[00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.520]   and then re-recognizing it when it's about to head out the door. Even if it has been
[00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.760]   transformed in some way, such as broken into network packets for transmission, zipped,
[00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:12.320]   deliberately fragmented into pieces, been through a series of global substitutions to mask it,
[00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.360]   or even, I think, encrypted. See here for more.
[00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.320]   It seems the most popular applications of this technology are intellectual property protection,
[00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:25.600]   leak prevention, and compliance. Companies focused on these applications include Next,
[00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:32.000]   Port Authority Technologies, Next, CodeGreenNetworksNext, TizorNext, Vontu. For more background on
[00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.560]   these types of applications, check out this article. I know another company using similar
[00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.120]   fingerprinting technology for a very different application. Palamida effectively sells an open
[00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.600]   source detector to software companies, so instead of using the technology to first crawl sensitive
[00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.600]   corporate documents and then sniff for them on the way out, Palamida themselves go crawl
[00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.160]   every open source repository they can find. Then, they call up the VP of Engineering,
[00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.240]   which is particularly fun during a proposed acquisition, and say, "Are you sure that
[00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.640]   none of your engineers have? Ever incorporated a bit of open source code that they shouldn't have
[00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.960]   and put it in your product and potentially per the GPL, therefore turns your entire product
[00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.120]   into open source?" Content fingerprinting technology also seems to be a great way for
[00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.120]   publishers to crawl the internet and look for scraped, stolen, or otherwise misappropriated
[00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:19.920]   content. I'm sure publishers do some of this today, but my guess is they are using more basic
[00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.680]   methods, which means they could be missing a lot. Speaking of publishers whose content
[00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.680]   has been misappropriated or scraped for someone else's profit, look at what these guys did with
[00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.880]   my soccer post. That's this post, scraped, with some irrelevant tags added, with ads alongside
[00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:37.120]   that are generating revenue for someone else. I'm not sure if it's illegal, but it's certainly not
[00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:41.440]   my intent to have someone else scrape my posts and effectively sell them. This audio version was
[00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.540]   created by Grant Duncan and AI, with permission from Dave.
