[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.360]   This is a Kell blog post from October 9th, 2006 titled "Sequoia Hits Again.
[00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.840]   YouTube Cells for 1.65b." If you somehow haven't heard, Google today
[00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.640]   announced that it will acquire YouTube in a stop transaction worth 1.65b. The official
[00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.880]   press release is here. The New York Times has an excellent article on the transaction.
[00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:23.600]   I've pulled a few excerpts related to YouTube and MarkLogic investor Sequoia Capital below.
[00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.000]   Sequoia's stake in YouTube has been estimated at approximately 30%, which means it would be
[00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.120]   worth about $4.95 million based on the acquisition price. Experts say Sequoia's go-it-alone investment
[00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.920]   in YouTube represents the kind of aggressive move for which Sequoia is known. A more typical and
[00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.680]   safer approach would have been to bring in other investors. Per their corporate boilerplate,
[00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.040]   YouTube was founded in February 2005, making the company 18 months old. This means that YouTube
[00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.840]   has created slightly less than 100M per month in value for its owners during its brief existence.
[00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.080]   I'm not positive, but I'd have to bet that this is a record rate of equity value creation.
[00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:59.920]   I must say that I'm pleased to have Sequoia as the lead investor in MarkLogic and that they
[00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.360]   continue to impress me not only with their day-to-day support of our company,
[00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.600]   e.g. introductions, advice, board participation, but also with their vision,
[00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.160]   e.g. brainstorming, thought leadership, and also their execution. On execution,
[00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:17.440]   three miracle transactions come to mind. Next, a tremendously successful IPO with Google in the
[00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:24.480]   middle A&I. PO Drought 24. Next, a 1.65b exit with YouTube in the middle of a general exit drought,
[00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.160]   soft software consolidation, which is mostly a big eat big phenomenon today.
[00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:33.280]   Next, doing the same thing with PayPal for other 1.5b in the bleakest part of the internet bust,
[00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.320]   2002, visit Sequoia here to learn more. I particularly like the left column of this
[00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.440]   page where they explain what they think makes for sustainable companies.
[00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:46.480]   Update, T-Ting 12206, here's a link to a Mercury News article that profiles 06,
[00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:50.800]   here's a link to a Mercury News article that profiles other major Sequoia success stories.
[00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.040]   This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI with permission from Dave.
