[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.640]   This is a Kehl blog post from October 17th, 2006 titled "Stock Options. It's a darn shame."
[00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.400]   It's a darn shame what's happened to stock options. When I first started in Silicon Valley
[00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.360]   in the 1980s, stock options were seen as a real innovation, a creative way to align employees
[00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.960]   with shareholders, a way to help employees participate in the value they were helping
[00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.280]   to create. Ever since then, and even now, I have remained a believer in stock options.
[00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.760]   Then a number of things went wrong, greed, the internet bubble, stock option expensing,
[00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.560]   and now the backdating scandals that are making headlines every day. To me, greed is the E1
[00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.400]   culprit. There's nothing wrong with stock options, per se. There is something wrong
[00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.960]   with boards who enable executives to make annual packages in the tens or hundreds of millions of
[00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:45.600]   dollars. While I know this sounds a bit like the guns don't kill people, people kill people,
[00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.280]   argument, stock options aren't the problem, corporate governance is.
[00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.920]   Then the internet bubble exposed the rising tide lists all ships. Problem with stock options. You
[00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:58.560]   could be an average CEO, doing an average job, running your average company, and if Wall Street
[00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:03.040]   5X distocks in your category, then you made a fortune. That people don't like this exposes
[00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.920]   a great irony. On one hand, as shareholders we just want the shares to go up, bur e g,
[00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.680]   I don't want excuses, I don't want to hear about enablers like product quality and customer
[00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.640]   satisfaction, I want results. On the other, when the shares go up simply because everyone else's
[00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.680]   did too, we don't like it. Even if you feel it's irrational to solve it, there is a simple solution
[00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.560]   to the rising tide problem, link the option strike price to a stock market index or a market basket
[00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:32.000]   of competitors. But that's not what happened. Because CEOs were greedy and boards let them be,
[00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.880]   the absolute numbers got out of control. So rather than fix the actual problem with options,
[00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:41.040]   the country decided to throw the stock options baby out with the rising tide bathwater,
[00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:46.400]   thus was born stock option expensing. I think stock option expensing is a bad idea. Why?
[00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.880]   Next, I majored in math at Berkeley and I can barely understand the Black Scholes model,
[00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.200]   so I can't imagine why anyone would want costs, calculated using it in a P&L statement.
[00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.080]   Next, a P&L statement should include real, known revenues and costs or simple allocations thereof,
[00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:04.880]   e.g., it makes sense to depreciate the cost of a factory that lasts 30 years over its 30-year life.
[00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.120]   I don't like including probabilistic costs into a P&L and think it's inaccurate to do so.
[00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.640]   Next, expensing options has the ironic consequence of making stock options less
[00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.480]   democconquence of making stock options less democratic. If you were trying to solve the
[00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.120]   executive of EGE, it makes sense to depreciate the cost of a factory that lasts due to years
[00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.560]   over its 30 years, over its 30 years, over its 30-year life. E agreed problem, you didn't solve it.
[00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:30.320]   The typical corporate response to option expensing has been to continue granting options,
[00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.040]   and/or restricted stock, to top management and to stop granting options to rank and file employees.
[00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.320]   So the fix actually made the concentration problem worse, not better.
[00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.480]   Next, eliminating rank and file stock options eliminates one of the few ways regular people
[00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.960]   can have economic class nobility. That leaves winning the lottery, successful stock and real
[00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.200]   estate speculation, and founding a company as the primary path to the rags-to-rags-to-riches
[00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.920]   part of the rags-to-rags-to-riches part of the American dream.
[00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:59.520]   In fact, the only thing I like about stock option expensing is that it gives a competitive advantage
[00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:05.120]   to startups, like MarkLogic. Because startup strike prices tend to be low, the total value
[00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:10.320]   of the grant strike price X shares tends to be small, thus the total expense tends to be small.
[00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.000]   What's more, few startups are measured on gap net income anyway.
[00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.800]   If stock options didn't have enough problems, now we have the backdating scandals.
[00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:20.640]   These seem to fall into two groups.
[00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:25.360]   Next, slobbiness. Companies with poor administration would fail to legally approve
[00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.480]   options on the date they intended, and thus would either knowingly or accidentally backdate them.
[00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.080]   Accidental backdating could happen, for example, when an option was granted by
[00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.640]   unanimous poor consent, which is effective not on the date the consent is sent for signature,
[00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.200]   but on the date the last consent is signed. Next, fraud greed. Some companies apparently
[00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:46.400]   set option dates back in time to build more profit into their grants.
[00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:51.200]   There is nothing illegal about pre-vesting shares in stock option grants, e.g., you.
[00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:55.840]   Can start out 248-svested, and there is nothing illegal about granting in-the-money options.
[00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:59.920]   It is illegal, however, to backdate options before their actual grant dates and to pretend
[00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:03.360]   that in-the-money grants, which have immediate tax consequences, were not.
[00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:06.720]   These things are all bad, but let's not blame stock options themselves.
[00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.600]   Let's blame corporate governance, greed, and sloppiness.
[00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.160]   Stock options are good. What many companies have done with them is bad. Let's separate the two.
[00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.400]   This audio version was created by Grant Duncan and AI with permission from Dave.
